<section class="hero">
  <h1>choudoufu <img class="hero-icon" src="choudoufu-inline-64.png" alt="" width="64" height="64"></h1>
  <p class="tagline">Ownership on the resource.</p>
  <p class="versions">Latest release <strong>v0.1.0</strong>, built on
  <strong>OpenTofu 1.13.0</strong> (fork point
  <a href="https://github.com/opentofu/opentofu/commit/03743ce6e8"><code>03743ce6e8</code></a>).</p>
</section>

<section class="lead">
  <p>choudoufu is OpenTofu with live resource markers. There is no state
  file, no backend and no lock. Ownership of a resource lives on the
  resource itself, as tags, and every plan rebuilds prior state from the
  live system instead of trusting a file. It is experimental and AWS only
  at the moment.</p>
  <p>Choudoufu state is allowed to be stale. OpenTofu state is not. A
  record here is rebuilt from the live system on demand, so a stale or
  missing record costs one re-read, never a wrong plan, and there is
  nothing to store, lock, back up, or repair.</p>
  <p>Everything else in this fork is stock OpenTofu, unmodified. New here?
  Start with the <a href="faq.html">FAQ</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="demo">
  <h2>See it work</h2>
  <p>One command runs the full demo against a local AWS emulator. It needs
  Docker. The exit code is the verdict.</p>
  <pre><code>bash stateless/e2e/run.sh --expect 5</code></pre>
</section>

